  "name": "Question 14",
  "problem": "Evaluate ${\\int }_{-2}^{0}x{e}^{-x}\\,\\text{d}x.$",
  "worked_solution": "Let's use integration by parts. We set\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=x & \u21d2 & {u}^{\u2032}=1 \\\\ {v}^{\u2032}={e}^{-x} & \u21d2 & v=-{e}^{-x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into the integration by parts formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int u{v}^{\u2032}\\,\\text{d}x & =uv-\\int v{u}^{\u2032}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int x\\cdot {e}^{-x}\\,\\text{d}x & =x\\cdot (-{e}^{-x})-\\int (-{e}^{-x})\\cdot 1\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the integral and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-2}^{0}x{e}^{-x}\\,\\text{d}x & =-x{e}^{-x}{|}_{-2}^{0}+{\\int }_{-2}^{0}{e}^{-x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-x{e}^{-x}{|}_{-2}^{0}-{e}^{-x}{|}_{-2}^{0} \\\\  & =[-0{e}^{-0}-2{e}^{2}]-[{e}^{0}-{e}^{2}] \\\\  & =-2{e}^{2}-1+{e}^{2} \\\\  & =-1-{e}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
